# A name-keyed Python builtin used as a VALUE rather than called.
#
# `abs`, `ord`, `chr`, `round`, `ascii`, `hash` and `id` are intercepted by NAME
# at the call site and routed to a pylib helper (pyabs_v, pyord_v, ...). A
# name-keyed intrinsic has no symbol for a bare mention to find, so every value
# spelling was `undefined variable (abs)` -- while `len`, a real proc under its
# own Python name, worked in all of them.
# bug-n-abs-is-not-a-value-while-len-and-str-are
#
# THE SHAPES ARE THE POINT: there are TWO doors into a callable value --
# PyMakeFuncValue (assignment) and the NilPy factor chain (an ARGUMENT) -- and
# wiring only the first made `f = abs; map(f, xs)` work while `map(abs, xs)`,
# the ticket's own headline repro, still refused. A fixture carrying only the
# assignment spelling would have certified that half-fix.
#
# hash/id are NOT compared against CPython by value: both are
# implementation-defined in Python and ours legitimately differ (CPython's
# hash(7) is 7; id is an address). The only thing that can be required of them
# is that the VALUE form agrees with the CALL form, which is what is asserted.
#
# Valid CPython, so the .expected beside this file is captured from the oracle.

# ---- door 1: assignment, then call through the value ------------------------
f = abs
print(f(-5), f(-5.5))
g = ord
h = chr
print(g("a"), h(66))
r = round
print(r(1.6), r(1.4))
a = ascii
print(a("q"))

# ---- door 2: the bare name as an ARGUMENT, never assigned -------------------
# This is the spelling that stayed broken when only door 1 was wired.
print(list(map(abs, [-5, 2, -9])))
print(list(map(ord, ["b", "a"])))
print(list(map(chr, [66, 65])))
print(sorted([-5, 2, -9], key=abs))

# ---- door 1 feeding door 2: the value, mapped -------------------------------
k = abs
print(list(map(k, [-3, 4])))

# ---- hash and id: agreement between the two forms, not a value -------------
x = "hi"
hf = hash
idf = id
print(hash(x) == hf(x), id(x) == idf(x))
print([hash(v) == hf(v) for v in [1, "a", 2.5]])

# ---- the CALL forms, unchanged: proves the value path was added beside the
# ---- name-keyed intercepts rather than in place of them.
print(abs(-5), ord("a"), chr(66), round(1.6), ascii("q"))

# ---- a user def still SHADOWS the builtin, in BOTH positions ----------------
# Python's own rule, and the call side spells it `procIdx < 0`. If this row ever
# prints 5 the value path has started outranking user code.
def abs(x):
    return 99
u = abs
print(u(-5), abs(-5))
